---@field name? aws.ecs.String The name of the gateway.
---@field status? aws.ecs.GatewayStatus The status of the gateway. A newly created gateway is always ACTIVE.

---The configuration for creating a Amazon ECS Managed Instances provider. This specifies how Amazon ECS should manage Amazon EC2 instances, including the infrastructure role, instance launch template, and whether to propagate tags from the capacity provider to the instances.
---@class aws.ecs.CreateManagedInstancesProviderConfiguration
---@field infrastructureRoleArn aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the infrastructure role that Amazon ECS uses to manage instances on your behalf. This role must have permissions to launch, terminate, and manage Amazon EC2 instances, as well as access to other Amazon Web Services services required for Amazon ECS Managed Instances functionality.
---@field instanceLaunchTemplate aws.ecs.InstanceLaunchTemplate The launch template configuration that specifies how Amazon ECS should launch Amazon EC2 instances. This includes the instance profile, network configuration, storage settings, and instance requirements for attribute-based instance type selection.
---@field propagateTags? aws.ecs.PropagateMITags|string Specifies whether to propagate tags from the capacity provider to the Amazon ECS Managed Instances. When enabled, tags applied to the capacity provider are automatically applied to all instances launched by this provider.
---@field infrastructureOptimization? aws.ecs.InfrastructureOptimization Defines how Amazon ECS Managed Instances optimizes the infrastastructure in your capacity provider. Provides control over the delay between when EC2 instances become idle or underutilized and when Amazon ECS optimizes them.
---@field autoRepairConfiguration? aws.ecs.AutoRepairConfiguration The auto repair configuration for the Amazon ECS Managed Instances capacity provider. Use this to enable or disable automatic replacement of container instances that are detected as unhealthy.

---@class aws.ecs.CreateServiceRequest
---@field cluster? aws.ecs.String The short name or full Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the cluster that you run your service on. If you do not specify a cluster, the default cluster is assumed.
---@field serviceName aws.ecs.String The name of your service. Up to 255 letters (uppercase and lowercase), numbers, underscores, and hyphens are allowed. Service names must be unique within a cluster, but you can have similarly named services in multiple clusters within a Region or across multiple Regions.
---@field taskDefinition? aws.ecs.String The family and revision (family:revision) or full ARN of the task definition to run in your service. If a revision isn't specified, the latest ACTIVE revision is used.
---@field availabilityZoneRebalancing? aws.ecs.AvailabilityZoneRebalancing|string Indicates whether to use Availability Zone rebalancing for the service.
---@field loadBalancers? aws.ecs.LoadBalancers A load balancer object representing the load balancers to use with your service. For more information, see Service load balancing in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field serviceRegistries? aws.ecs.ServiceRegistries The details of the service discovery registry to associate with this service. For more information, see Service discovery.
---@field desiredCount? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The number of instantiations of the specified task definition to place and keep running in your service.
---@field clientToken? aws.ecs.String An identifier that you provide to ensure the idempotency of the request. It must be unique and is case sensitive. Up to 36 ASCII characters in the range of 33-126 (inclusive) are allowed.
---@field launchType? aws.ecs.LaunchType|string The infrastructure that you run your service on. For more information, see Amazon ECS launch types in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field capacityProviderStrategy? aws.ecs.CapacityProviderStrategy The capacity provider strategy to use for the service.
---@field platformVersion? aws.ecs.String The platform version that your tasks in the service are running on. A platform version is specified only for tasks using the Fargate launch type. If one isn't specified, the LATEST platform version is used. For more information, see Fargate platform versions in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field role? aws.ecs.String The name or full Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the IAM role that allows Amazon ECS to make calls to your load balancer on your behalf. This parameter is only permitted if you are using a load balancer with your service and your task definition doesn't use the awsvpc network mode. If you specify the role parameter, you must also specify a load balancer object with the loadBalancers parameter.
---@field deploymentConfiguration? aws.ecs.DeploymentConfiguration Optional deployment parameters that control how many tasks run during the deployment and the ordering of stopping and starting tasks.
---@field placementConstraints? aws.ecs.PlacementConstraints An array of placement constraint objects to use for tasks in your service. You can specify a maximum of 10 constraints for each task. This limit includes constraints in the task definition and those specified at runtime.
---@field placementStrategy? aws.ecs.PlacementStrategies The placement strategy objects to use for tasks in your service. You can specify a maximum of 5 strategy rules for each service.
---@field networkConfiguration? aws.ecs.NetworkConfiguration The network configuration for the service. This parameter is required for task definitions that use the awsvpc network mode to receive their own elastic network interface, and it isn't supported for other network modes. For more information, see Task networking in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field healthCheckGracePeriodSeconds? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The period of time, in seconds, that the Amazon ECS service scheduler ignores unhealthy Elastic Load Balancing, VPC Lattice, and container health checks after a task has first started. If you do not specify a health check grace period value, the default value of 0 is used. If you do not use any of the health checks, then healthCheckGracePeriodSeconds is unused.
---@field schedulingStrategy? aws.ecs.SchedulingStrategy|string The scheduling strategy to use for the service. For more information, see Services.
---@field deploymentController? aws.ecs.DeploymentController The deployment controller to use for the service. If no deployment controller is specified, the default value of ECS is used.
---@field tags? aws.ecs.Tags The metadata that you apply to the service to help you categorize and organize them. Each tag consists of a key and an optional value, both of which you define. When a service is deleted, the tags are deleted as well.
---@field enableECSManagedTags? aws.ecs.Boolean Specifies whether to turn on Amazon ECS managed tags for the tasks within the service. For more information, see Tagging your Amazon ECS resources in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field propagateTags? aws.ecs.PropagateTags|string Specifies whether to propagate the tags from the task definition to the task. If no value is specified, the tags aren't propagated. Tags can only be propagated to the task during task creation. To add tags to a task after task creation, use the TagResource API action.
---@field enableExecuteCommand? aws.ecs.Boolean Determines whether the execute command functionality is turned on for the service. If true, this enables execute command functionality on all containers in the service tasks.
---@field serviceConnectConfiguration? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectConfiguration The configuration for this service to discover and connect to services, and be discovered by, and connected from, other services within a namespace.
---@field volumeConfigurations? aws.ecs.ServiceVolumeConfigurations The configuration for a volume specified in the task definition as a volume that is configured at launch time. Currently, the only supported volume type is an Amazon EBS volume.
---@field vpcLatticeConfigurations? aws.ecs.VpcLatticeConfigurations The VPC Lattice configuration for the service being created.
---@field gatewayConnectors? aws.ecs.GatewayConnectorList The gateway connectors that attach the service to a gateway ingress path. You can specify at most one gateway connector.
---@field monitoring? aws.ecs.MonitoringConfiguration The optional monitoring configuration for the service, which defines the resolution for the service-level CPUUtilization and MemoryUtilization Amazon CloudWatch metrics. When not specified, Amazon ECS uses the default resolution of 60 seconds.

---@class aws.ecs.CreateServiceResponse
---@field service? aws.ecs.Service The full description of your service following the create call.

---@class aws.ecs.CreateTaskSetRequest
---@field service aws.ecs.String The short name or full Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the service to create the task set in.
---@field cluster aws.ecs.String The short name or full Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the cluster that hosts the service to create the task set in.
---@field externalId? aws.ecs.String An optional non-unique tag that identifies this task set in external systems. If the task set is associated with a service discovery registry, the tasks in this task set will have the ECS_TASK_SET_EXTERNAL_ID Cloud Map attribute set to the provided value.
---@field taskDefinition aws.ecs.String The task definition for the tasks in the task set to use. If a revision isn't specified, the latest ACTIVE revision is used.
---@field networkConfiguration? aws.ecs.NetworkConfiguration An object representing the network configuration for a task set.
---@field loadBalancers? aws.ecs.LoadBalancers A load balancer object representing the load balancer to use with the task set. The supported load balancer types are either an Application Load Balancer or a Network Load Balancer.
---@field serviceRegistries? aws.ecs.ServiceRegistries The details of the service discovery registries to assign to this task set. For more information, see Service discovery.
---@field launchType? aws.ecs.LaunchType|string The launch type that new tasks in the task set uses. For more information, see Amazon ECS launch types in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field capacityProviderStrategy? aws.ecs.CapacityProviderStrategy The capacity provider strategy to use for the task set.
---@field platformVersion? aws.ecs.String The platform version that the tasks in the task set uses. A platform version is specified only for tasks using the Fargate launch type. If one isn't specified, the LATEST platform version is used.
---@field scale? aws.ecs.Scale A floating-point percentage of the desired number of tasks to place and keep running in the task set.
---@field clientToken? aws.ecs.String An identifier that you provide to ensure the idempotency of the request. It must be unique and is case sensitive. Up to 36 ASCII characters in the range of 33-126 (inclusive) are allowed.
---@field tags? aws.ecs.Tags The metadata that you apply to the task set to help you categorize and organize them. Each tag consists of a key and an optional value. You define both. When a service is deleted, the tags are deleted.

---@class aws.ecs.CreateTaskSetResponse
---@field taskSet? aws.ecs.TaskSet Information about a set of Amazon ECS tasks in either an CodeDeploy or an EXTERNAL deployment. A task set includes details such as the desired number of tasks, how many tasks are running, and whether the task set serves production traffic.

---The optional filter to narrow the ListServiceDeployment results.
---@class aws.ecs.CreatedAt
---@field before? aws.ecs.Timestamp Include service deployments in the result that were created before this time. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field after? aws.ecs.Timestamp Include service deployments in the result that were created after this time. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.

---The CloudWatch alarm configuration for a daemon. When enabled, CloudWatch alarms determine whether a daemon deployment has failed.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonAlarmConfiguration
---@field alarmNames? aws.ecs.StringList The CloudWatch alarm names to monitor during a daemon deployment.
---@field enable? aws.ecs.Boolean Determines whether to use the CloudWatch alarm option in the daemon deployment process. The default value is false.

---Information about a capacity provider associated with a daemon revision.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonCapacityProvider
---@field arn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the capacity provider.
---@field runningCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of daemon tasks running on this capacity provider.
---@field withoutDaemonCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of instances on this capacity provider that are running without the daemon task. This applies to daemons that aren't critical, where the instance remains available for your other tasks even if the daemon task can't start or stops. These instances aren't included in runningCount.

---@alias aws.ecs.DaemonCapacityProviderList aws.ecs.DaemonCapacityProvider[]

---Information about the circuit breaker used to determine when a daemon deployment has failed.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonCircuitBreaker
---@field failureCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of times the circuit breaker detected a daemon deployment failure.
---@field status? aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentRollbackMonitorsStatus The circuit breaker status. Amazon ECS is not using the circuit breaker for daemon deployment failures when the status is DISABLED.
---@field threshold aws.ecs.Integer The threshold which determines that the daemon deployment failed.

---A container definition for a daemon task. Daemon container definitions describe the containers that run as part of a daemon task on container instances managed by capacity providers.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonContainerDefinition
---@field name? aws.ecs.String The name of the container. Up to 255 letters (uppercase and lowercase), numbers, underscores, and hyphens are allowed.
---@field image aws.ecs.String The image used to start the container. This string is passed directly to the Docker daemon. Images in the Docker Hub registry are available by default. Other repositories are specified with either repository-url/image:tag or repository-url/image@digest .
---@field memory? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The amount (in MiB) of memory to present to the container. If the container attempts to exceed the memory specified here, the container is killed.
---@field memoryReservation? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The soft limit (in MiB) of memory to reserve for the container.
---@field repositoryCredentials? aws.ecs.RepositoryCredentials|distilled.Secret The private repository authentication credentials to use.
---@field healthCheck? aws.ecs.HealthCheck The container health check command and associated configuration parameters for the container.
---@field cpu? aws.ecs.Integer The number of cpu units reserved for the container.
---@field essential? aws.ecs.BoxedBoolean If the essential parameter of a container is marked as true, and that container fails or stops for any reason, all other containers that are part of the task are stopped.
---@field entryPoint? aws.ecs.StringList The entry point that's passed to the container.
---@field command? aws.ecs.StringList The command that's passed to the container.
---@field workingDirectory? aws.ecs.String The working directory to run commands inside the container in.
---@field environmentFiles? aws.ecs.EnvironmentFiles A list of files containing the environment variables to pass to a container.
---@field environment? aws.ecs.EnvironmentVariables|distilled.Secret The environment variables to pass to a container.
---@field secrets? aws.ecs.SecretList The secrets to pass to the container.
---@field readonlyRootFilesystem? aws.ecs.BoxedBoolean When this parameter is true, the container is given read-only access to its root file system.
---@field mountPoints? aws.ecs.MountPointList The mount points for data volumes in your container.
---@field logConfiguration? aws.ecs.LogConfiguration The log configuration specification for the container.
---@field firelensConfiguration? aws.ecs.FirelensConfiguration The FireLens configuration for the container. This is used to specify and configure a log router for container logs.
---@field privileged? aws.ecs.BoxedBoolean When this parameter is true, the container is given elevated privileges on the host container instance (similar to the root user).
---@field user? aws.ecs.String The user to use inside the container.
---@field ulimits? aws.ecs.UlimitList A list of ulimits to set in the container.
---@field linuxParameters? aws.ecs.DaemonLinuxParameters Linux-specific modifications that are applied to the container configuration, such as Linux kernel capabilities.
---@field dependsOn? aws.ecs.ContainerDependencies The dependencies defined for container startup and shutdown. A container can contain multiple dependencies on other containers in a task definition.
---@field startTimeout? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger Time duration (in seconds) to wait before giving up on resolving dependencies for a container.
---@field stopTimeout? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger Time duration (in seconds) to wait before the container is forcefully killed if it doesn't exit normally on its own.
---@field systemControls? aws.ecs.SystemControls A list of namespaced kernel parameters to set in the container.
---@field interactive? aws.ecs.BoxedBoolean When this parameter is true, you can deploy containerized applications that require stdin or a tty to be allocated.
---@field pseudoTerminal? aws.ecs.BoxedBoolean When this parameter is true, a TTY is allocated.
---@field restartPolicy? aws.ecs.ContainerRestartPolicy The restart policy for the container. When you set up a restart policy, Amazon ECS can restart the container without needing to replace the task.

---@alias aws.ecs.DaemonContainerDefinitionList aws.ecs.DaemonContainerDefinition[]

---The details about the container image a daemon revision uses.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonContainerImage
---@field containerName? aws.ecs.String The name of the container.
---@field imageDigest? aws.ecs.String The container image digest.
---@field image? aws.ecs.String The container image.

---@alias aws.ecs.DaemonContainerImages aws.ecs.DaemonContainerImage[]

---Information about a daemon deployment. A daemon deployment orchestrates the progressive rollout of daemon task updates across container instances.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonDeployment
---@field daemonDeploymentArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the daemon deployment.
---@field clusterArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the cluster that hosts the daemon.
---@field status? aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentStatus The status of the daemon deployment.
---@field statusReason? aws.ecs.String Information about why the daemon deployment is in the current status.
---@field targetDaemonRevision? aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentRevisionDetail The daemon revision being deployed.
---@field sourceDaemonRevisions? aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentRevisionDetailList The currently deployed daemon revisions that are being replaced.
---@field circuitBreaker? aws.ecs.DaemonCircuitBreaker The circuit breaker configuration that determines when a daemon deployment has failed.
---@field alarms? aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentAlarms The CloudWatch alarms that determine when a daemon deployment fails.
---@field rollback? aws.ecs.DaemonRollback The rollback options for the daemon deployment.
---@field deploymentConfiguration? aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentConfiguration The deployment configuration used for this daemon deployment.
---@field createdAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the daemon deployment was created. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field startedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the daemon deployment started. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field stoppedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the daemon deployment stopped. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field finishedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the daemon deployment finished. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.

---The CloudWatch alarms used to determine a daemon deployment failed.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentAlarms
---@field status? aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentRollbackMonitorsStatus The status of the alarms check. Amazon ECS is not using alarms for daemon deployment failures when the status is DISABLED.
---@field alarmNames? aws.ecs.StringList The name of the CloudWatch alarms that determine when a daemon deployment failed.
---@field triggeredAlarmNames? aws.ecs.StringList One or more CloudWatch alarm names that have been triggered during the daemon deployment.

---Information about a capacity provider during a daemon deployment.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentCapacityProvider
---@field arn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the capacity provider.
---@field runningInstanceCount? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The number of instances running daemon tasks on this capacity provider.
---@field withoutDaemonInstanceCount? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The number of instances on this capacity provider that are running without the daemon task. This applies to daemons that aren't critical, where the instance remains available for your other tasks even if the daemon task can't start or stops. These instances aren't included in runningInstanceCount.
---@field drainingInstanceCount? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The number of instances being drained on this capacity provider during the deployment.

---@alias aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentCapacityProviderList aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentCapacityProvider[]

---Optional deployment parameters that control how a daemon rolls out updates across container instances.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentConfiguration
---@field drainPercent? aws.ecs.DaemonDrainPercent The percentage of container instances to drain simultaneously during a daemon deployment. Valid values are between 0.0 and 100.0.
---@field alarms? aws.ecs.DaemonAlarmConfiguration The CloudWatch alarm configuration for the daemon deployment. When alarms are triggered during a deployment, the deployment can be automatically rolled back.
---@field bakeTimeInMinutes? aws.ecs.Integer The amount of time (in minutes) to wait after a successful deployment step before proceeding. This allows time to monitor for issues before continuing. The default value is 0.

---@alias aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentList aws.ecs.DaemonDeployment[]

---Details about a daemon revision during a deployment, including running and draining instance counts per capacity provider.
---@class aws.ecs.DaemonDeploymentRevisionDetail
---@field arn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the daemon revision.
