---The instance requirements for attribute-based instance type selection. Instead of specifying exact instance types, you define requirements such as vCPU count, memory size, network performance, and accelerator specifications. Amazon ECS automatically selects Amazon EC2 instance types that match these requirements, providing flexibility and helping to mitigate capacity constraints.
---@class aws.ecs.InstanceRequirementsRequest
---@field vCpuCount aws.ecs.VCpuCountRangeRequest The minimum and maximum number of vCPUs for the instance types. Amazon ECS selects instance types that have vCPU counts within this range.
---@field memoryMiB aws.ecs.MemoryMiBRequest The minimum and maximum amount of memory in mebibytes (MiB) for the instance types. Amazon ECS selects instance types that have memory within this range.
---@field cpuManufacturers? aws.ecs.CpuManufacturerSet The CPU manufacturers to include or exclude. You can specify intel, amd, or amazon-web-services to control which CPU types are used for your workloads.
---@field memoryGiBPerVCpu? aws.ecs.MemoryGiBPerVCpuRequest The minimum and maximum amount of memory per vCPU in gibibytes (GiB). This helps ensure that instance types have the appropriate memory-to-CPU ratio for your workloads.
---@field excludedInstanceTypes? aws.ecs.ExcludedInstanceTypeSet The instance types to exclude from selection. Use this to prevent Amazon ECS from selecting specific instance types that may not be suitable for your workloads.
---@field instanceGenerations? aws.ecs.InstanceGenerationSet The instance generations to include. You can specify current to use the latest generation instances, or previous to include previous generation instances for cost optimization.
---@field spotMaxPricePercentageOverLowestPrice? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The maximum price for Spot instances as a percentage over the lowest priced On-Demand instance. This helps control Spot instance costs while maintaining access to capacity.
