---@field placementConstraints? aws.ecs.PlacementConstraints The placement constraints for the tasks in the service.
---@field placementStrategy? aws.ecs.PlacementStrategies The placement strategy that determines how tasks for the service are placed.
---@field networkConfiguration? aws.ecs.NetworkConfiguration The VPC subnet and security group configuration for tasks that receive their own elastic network interface by using the awsvpc networking mode.
---@field healthCheckGracePeriodSeconds? aws.ecs.BoxedInteger The period of time, in seconds, that the Amazon ECS service scheduler ignores unhealthy Elastic Load Balancing, VPC Lattice, and container health checks after a task has first started.
---@field schedulingStrategy? aws.ecs.SchedulingStrategy The scheduling strategy to use for the service. For more information, see Services.
---@field deploymentController? aws.ecs.DeploymentController The deployment controller type the service is using.
---@field tags? aws.ecs.Tags The metadata that you apply to the service to help you categorize and organize them. Each tag consists of a key and an optional value. You define bot the key and value.
---@field createdBy? aws.ecs.String The principal that created the service.
---@field enableECSManagedTags aws.ecs.Boolean Determines whether to use Amazon ECS managed tags for the tasks in the service. For more information, see Tagging Your Amazon ECS Resources in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field propagateTags? aws.ecs.PropagateTags Determines whether to propagate the tags from the task definition or the service to the task. If no value is specified, the tags aren't propagated.
---@field enableExecuteCommand aws.ecs.Boolean Determines whether the execute command functionality is turned on for the service. If true, the execute command functionality is turned on for all containers in tasks as part of the service.
---@field availabilityZoneRebalancing? aws.ecs.AvailabilityZoneRebalancing Indicates whether to use Availability Zone rebalancing for the service.
---@field resourceManagementType? aws.ecs.ResourceManagementType Identifies whether an ECS Service is an Express Service managed by ECS, or managed by the customer. The valid values are ECS and CUSTOMER

---Configuration for Service Connect access logging. Access logs provide detailed information about requests made to your service, including request patterns, response codes, and timing data for debugging and monitoring purposes.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectAccessLogConfiguration
---@field format aws.ecs.ServiceConnectAccessLoggingFormat|string The format for Service Connect access log output. Choose TEXT for human-readable logs or JSON for structured data that integrates well with log analysis tools.
---@field includeQueryParameters? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectIncludeQueryParameters|string Specifies whether to include query parameters in Service Connect access logs.

---The format for Service Connect access log output. Choose TEXT for human-readable logs or JSON for structured data that integrates well with log analysis tools.
---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceConnectAccessLoggingFormat "TEXT"|"JSON"

---Each alias ("endpoint") is a fully-qualified name and port number that other tasks ("clients") can use to connect to this service.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectClientAlias
---@field port aws.ecs.PortNumber The listening port number for the Service Connect proxy. This port is available inside of all of the tasks within the same namespace.
---@field dnsName? aws.ecs.String The dnsName is the name that you use in the applications of client tasks to connect to this service. The name must be a valid DNS name but doesn't need to be fully-qualified. The name can include up to 127 characters. The name can include lowercase letters, numbers, underscores (_), hyphens (-), and periods (.). The name can't start with a hyphen.
---@field testTrafficRules? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTestTrafficRules The configuration for test traffic routing rules used during blue/green deployments with Amazon ECS Service Connect. This allows you to route a portion of traffic to the new service revision of your service for testing before shifting all production traffic.

---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceConnectClientAliasList aws.ecs.ServiceConnectClientAlias[]

---The Service Connect configuration of your Amazon ECS service. The configuration for this service to discover and connect to services, and be discovered by, and connected from, other services within a namespace.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectConfiguration
---@field enabled aws.ecs.Boolean Specifies whether to use Service Connect with this service.
---@field namespace? aws.ecs.String The namespace name or full Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the Cloud Map namespace for use with Service Connect. The namespace must be in the same Amazon Web Services Region as the Amazon ECS service and cluster. The type of namespace doesn't affect Service Connect. For more information about Cloud Map, see Working with Services in the Cloud Map Developer Guide.
---@field services? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectServiceList The list of Service Connect service objects. These are names and aliases (also known as endpoints) that are used by other Amazon ECS services to connect to this service.
---@field logConfiguration? aws.ecs.LogConfiguration
---@field accessLogConfiguration? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectAccessLogConfiguration The configuration for Service Connect access logging. Access logs capture detailed information about requests made to your service, including request patterns, response codes, and timing data. They can be useful for debugging connectivity issues, monitoring service performance, and auditing service-to-service communication for security and compliance purposes.
---@field containerConfigurations? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectContainerConfigurations The settings for the Service Connect proxy container that Amazon ECS adds to each task in this service. Each object in the list sets exactly one setting, and each setting can appear only once in the list.

---Contains a configuration setting for the Service Connect proxy container, such as the log level. Specify exactly one member in each object.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectContainerConfiguration
---@field logLevel? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectContainerLogLevel|string The log level of the Service Connect proxy container. Set the log level to debug to collect more detailed proxy logs when you troubleshoot connectivity issues between services, and set it back to info when you finish. Debug logging increases the volume of logs that the proxy container writes. If you don't specify a log level, the default is info.

---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceConnectContainerConfigurations aws.ecs.ServiceConnectContainerConfiguration[]

---The log level of the Service Connect proxy container.
---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceConnectContainerLogLevel "debug"|"info"

---Controls whether query parameters are included in Service Connect access logs. Consider security and privacy implications when enabling this feature. By default, this parameter is DISABLED.
---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceConnectIncludeQueryParameters "DISABLED"|"ENABLED"

---The Service Connect service object configuration. For more information, see Service Connect in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectService
---@field portName aws.ecs.String The portName must match the name of one of the portMappings from all the containers in the task definition of this Amazon ECS service.
---@field discoveryName? aws.ecs.String The discoveryName is the name of the new Cloud Map service that Amazon ECS creates for this Amazon ECS service. This must be unique within the Cloud Map namespace. The name can contain up to 64 characters. The name can include lowercase letters, numbers, underscores (_), and hyphens (-). The name can't start with a hyphen.
---@field clientAliases? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectClientAliasList The list of client aliases for this Service Connect service. You use these to assign names that can be used by client applications. The maximum number of client aliases that you can have in this list is 1.
---@field ingressPortOverride? aws.ecs.PortNumber The port number for the Service Connect proxy to listen on.
---@field timeout? aws.ecs.TimeoutConfiguration A reference to an object that represents the configured timeouts for Service Connect.
---@field tls? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTlsConfiguration A reference to an object that represents a Transport Layer Security (TLS) configuration.

---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceConnectServiceList aws.ecs.ServiceConnectService[]

---The Service Connect resource. Each configuration maps a discovery name to a Cloud Map service name. The data is stored in Cloud Map as part of the Service Connect configuration for each discovery name of this Amazon ECS service.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectServiceResource
---@field discoveryName? aws.ecs.String The discovery name of this Service Connect resource.
---@field discoveryArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) for the service in Cloud Map that matches the discovery name for this Service Connect resource. You can use this ARN in other integrations with Cloud Map. However, Service Connect can't ensure connectivity outside of Amazon ECS.

---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceConnectServiceResourceList aws.ecs.ServiceConnectServiceResource[]

---The header matching rules for test traffic routing in Amazon ECS blue/green deployments. These rules determine how incoming requests are matched based on HTTP headers to route test traffic to the new service revision.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTestTrafficHeaderMatchRules
---@field exact aws.ecs.String The exact value that the HTTP header must match for the test traffic routing rule to apply. This provides precise control over which requests are routed to the new service revision during blue/green deployments.

---The HTTP header rules used to identify and route test traffic during Amazon ECS blue/green deployments. These rules specify which HTTP headers to examine and what values to match for routing decisions.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTestTrafficHeaderRules
---@field name aws.ecs.String The name of the HTTP header to examine for test traffic routing. Common examples include custom headers like X-Test-Version or X-Canary-Request that can be used to identify test traffic.
---@field value? aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTestTrafficHeaderMatchRules The header value matching configuration that determines how the HTTP header value is evaluated for test traffic routing decisions.

---The test traffic routing configuration for Amazon ECS blue/green deployments. This configuration allows you to define rules for routing specific traffic to the new service revision during the deployment process, allowing for safe testing before full production traffic shift.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTestTrafficRules
---@field header aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTestTrafficHeaderRules The HTTP header-based routing rules that determine which requests should be routed to the new service version during blue/green deployment testing. These rules provide fine-grained control over test traffic routing based on request headers.

---The certificate root authority that secures your service.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTlsCertificateAuthority
---@field awsPcaAuthorityArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the Amazon Web Services Private Certificate Authority certificate.

---The key that encrypts and decrypts your resources for Service Connect TLS.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTlsConfiguration
---@field issuerCertificateAuthority aws.ecs.ServiceConnectTlsCertificateAuthority The signer certificate authority.
---@field kmsKey? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Web Services Key Management Service key.
---@field roleArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the IAM role that's associated with the Service Connect TLS.

---The summary of the current service revision configuration
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceCurrentRevisionSummary
---@field arn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the current service revision.
---@field requestedTaskCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of requested tasks in the current service revision
---@field runningTaskCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of running tasks of the current service revision
---@field pendingTaskCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of pending tasks in the current service revision

---@alias aws.ecs.ServiceCurrentRevisionSummaryList aws.ecs.ServiceCurrentRevisionSummary[]

---Information about the service deployment.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceDeployment
---@field serviceDeploymentArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the service deployment.
---@field serviceArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the service for this service deployment.
---@field clusterArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the cluster that hosts the service.
---@field createdAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the service deployment was created. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field startedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the service deployment statred. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field finishedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the service deployment finished. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field stoppedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time the service deployment stopped. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field updatedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time that the service deployment was last updated. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field sourceServiceRevisions? aws.ecs.ServiceRevisionsSummaryList The currently deployed workload configuration.
---@field targetServiceRevision? aws.ecs.ServiceRevisionSummary The workload configuration being deployed.
---@field status? aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentStatus The service deployment state.
---@field statusReason? aws.ecs.String Information about why the service deployment is in the current status. For example, the circuit breaker detected a failure.
---@field lifecycleStage? aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentLifecycleStage The current lifecycle stage of the deployment. Possible values include:
---@field lifecycleHookDetails? aws.ecs.DeploymentLifecycleHookDetailList The details of the lifecycle hooks for the current service deployment.
---@field deploymentConfiguration? aws.ecs.DeploymentConfiguration
---@field rollback? aws.ecs.Rollback The rollback options the service deployment uses when the deployment fails.
---@field deploymentCircuitBreaker? aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentCircuitBreaker The circuit breaker configuration that determines a service deployment failed.
---@field alarms? aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentAlarms The CloudWatch alarms that determine when a service deployment fails.

---The CloudWatch alarms used to determine a service deployment failed.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentAlarms
---@field status? aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentRollbackMonitorsStatus The status of the alarms check. Amazon ECS is not using alarms for service deployment failures when the status is DISABLED.
---@field alarmNames? aws.ecs.StringList The name of the CloudWatch alarms that determine when a service deployment failed. A "," separates the alarms.
---@field triggeredAlarmNames? aws.ecs.StringList One or more CloudWatch alarm names that have been triggered during the service deployment. A "," separates the alarm names.

---The service deployment properties that are retured when you call ListServiceDeployments.
---@class aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentBrief
---@field serviceDeploymentArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the service deployment.
---@field serviceArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the service for this service deployment.
---@field clusterArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the cluster that hosts the service.
---@field startedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time that the service deployment statred. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field createdAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time that the service deployment was created. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field finishedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The time that the service deployment completed. The format is yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSSSS.
---@field targetServiceRevisionArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the service revision being deplyed.
---@field status? aws.ecs.ServiceDeploymentStatus The status of the service deployment
