---Details on a task in a cluster.
---@class aws.ecs.Task
---@field attachments? aws.ecs.Attachments The Elastic Network Adapter that's associated with the task if the task uses the awsvpc network mode.
---@field attributes? aws.ecs.Attributes The attributes of the task
---@field availabilityZone? aws.ecs.String The Availability Zone for the task.
---@field capacityProviderName? aws.ecs.String The capacity provider that's associated with the task.
---@field clusterArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the cluster that hosts the task.
---@field connectivity? aws.ecs.Connectivity The connectivity status of a task.
---@field connectivityAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task last went into CONNECTED status.
---@field containerInstanceArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the container instances that host the task.
---@field containers? aws.ecs.Containers The containers that's associated with the task.
---@field cpu? aws.ecs.String The number of CPU units used by the task as expressed in a task definition. It can be expressed as an integer using CPU units (for example, 1024). It can also be expressed as a string using vCPUs (for example, 1 vCPU or 1 vcpu). String values are converted to an integer that indicates the CPU units when the task definition is registered.
---@field createdAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task was created. More specifically, it's for the time when the task entered the PENDING state.
---@field desiredStatus? aws.ecs.String The desired status of the task. For more information, see Task Lifecycle.
---@field enableExecuteCommand aws.ecs.Boolean Determines whether execute command functionality is turned on for this task. If true, execute command functionality is turned on all the containers in the task.
---@field executionStoppedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task execution stopped.
---@field group? aws.ecs.String The name of the task group that's associated with the task.
---@field healthStatus? aws.ecs.HealthStatus The health status for the task. It's determined by the health of the essential containers in the task. If all essential containers in the task are reporting as HEALTHY, the task status also reports as HEALTHY. If any essential containers in the task are reporting as UNHEALTHY or UNKNOWN, the task status also reports as UNHEALTHY or UNKNOWN.
---@field inferenceAccelerators? aws.ecs.InferenceAccelerators The Elastic Inference accelerator that's associated with the task.
---@field lastStatus? aws.ecs.String The last known status for the task. For more information, see Task Lifecycle.
---@field launchType? aws.ecs.LaunchType The infrastructure where your task runs on. For more information, see Amazon ECS launch types in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field memory? aws.ecs.String The amount of memory (in MiB) that the task uses as expressed in a task definition. It can be expressed as an integer using MiB (for example, 1024). If it's expressed as a string using GB (for example, 1GB or 1 GB), it's converted to an integer indicating the MiB when the task definition is registered.
---@field overrides? aws.ecs.TaskOverride One or more container overrides.
---@field platformVersion? aws.ecs.String The platform version where your task runs on. A platform version is only specified for tasks that use the Fargate launch type. If you didn't specify one, the LATEST platform version is used. For more information, see Fargate Platform Versions in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field platformFamily? aws.ecs.String The operating system that your tasks are running on. A platform family is specified only for tasks that use the Fargate launch type.
---@field pullStartedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the container image pull began.
---@field pullStoppedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the container image pull completed.
---@field startedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task started. More specifically, it's for the time when the task transitioned from the PENDING state to the RUNNING state.
---@field startedBy? aws.ecs.String The tag specified when a task is started. If an Amazon ECS service started the task, the startedBy parameter contains the deployment ID of that service.
---@field stopCode? aws.ecs.TaskStopCode The stop code indicating why a task was stopped. The stoppedReason might contain additional details.
---@field stoppedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task was stopped. More specifically, it's for the time when the task transitioned from the RUNNING state to the STOPPED state.
---@field stoppedReason? aws.ecs.String The reason that the task was stopped.
---@field stoppingAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task stops. More specifically, it's for the time when the task transitions from the RUNNING state to STOPPING.
---@field tags? aws.ecs.Tags The metadata that you apply to the task to help you categorize and organize the task. Each tag consists of a key and an optional value. You define both the key and value.
---@field taskArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the task.
---@field taskDefinitionArn? aws.ecs.String The ARN of the task definition that creates the task.
---@field version aws.ecs.Long The version counter for the task. Every time a task experiences a change that starts a CloudWatch event, the version counter is incremented. If you replicate your Amazon ECS task state with CloudWatch Events, you can compare the version of a task reported by the Amazon ECS API actions with the version reported in CloudWatch Events for the task (inside the detail object) to verify that the version ...
---@field ephemeralStorage? aws.ecs.EphemeralStorage The ephemeral storage settings for the task.
---@field fargateEphemeralStorage? aws.ecs.TaskEphemeralStorage The Fargate ephemeral storage settings for the task.
---@field trustedExecutionSummary? aws.ecs.TrustedExecutionSummary A summary of the trusted execution configuration for the task, including the isolation mode. This is a read-only view of the configuration that was set on the task definition.

---The details of a task definition which describes the container and volume definitions of an Amazon Elastic Container Service task. You can specify which Docker images to use, the required resources, and other configurations related to launching the task definition through an Amazon ECS service or task.
---@class aws.ecs.TaskDefinition
---@field taskDefinitionArn? aws.ecs.String The full Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the task definition.
---@field containerDefinitions? aws.ecs.ContainerDefinitions A list of container definitions in JSON format that describe the different containers that make up your task. For more information about container definition parameters and defaults, see Amazon ECS Task Definitions in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field family? aws.ecs.String The name of a family that this task definition is registered to. Up to 255 characters are allowed. Letters (both uppercase and lowercase letters), numbers, hyphens (-), and underscores (_) are allowed.
---@field taskRoleArn? aws.ecs.String The short name or full Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the Identity and Access Management role that grants containers in the task permission to call Amazon Web Services APIs on your behalf. For informationabout the required IAM roles for Amazon ECS, see IAM roles for Amazon ECS in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field executionRoleArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the task execution role that grants the Amazon ECS container agent permission to make Amazon Web Services API calls on your behalf. For informationabout the required IAM roles for Amazon ECS, see IAM roles for Amazon ECS in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field networkMode? aws.ecs.NetworkMode The Docker networking mode to use for the containers in the task. The valid values are none, bridge, awsvpc, and host. If no network mode is specified, the default is bridge.
---@field revision aws.ecs.Integer The revision of the task in a particular family. The revision is a version number of a task definition in a family. When you register a task definition for the first time, the revision is 1. Each time that you register a new revision of a task definition in the same family, the revision value always increases by one. This is even if you deregistered previous revisions in this family.
---@field volumes? aws.ecs.VolumeList The list of data volume definitions for the task. For more information, see Using data volumes in tasks in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field status? aws.ecs.TaskDefinitionStatus The status of the task definition.
---@field requiresAttributes? aws.ecs.RequiresAttributes The container instance attributes required by your task. When an Amazon EC2 instance is registered to your cluster, the Amazon ECS container agent assigns some standard attributes to the instance. You can apply custom attributes. These are specified as key-value pairs using the Amazon ECS console or the PutAttributes API. These attributes are used when determining task placement for tasks hosted o...
---@field placementConstraints? aws.ecs.TaskDefinitionPlacementConstraints An array of placement constraint objects to use for tasks.
---@field compatibilities? aws.ecs.CompatibilityList Amazon ECS validates the task definition parameters with those supported by the launch type. For more information, see Amazon ECS launch types in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field runtimePlatform? aws.ecs.RuntimePlatform The operating system that your task definitions are running on. A platform family is specified only for tasks using the Fargate launch type.
---@field requiresCompatibilities? aws.ecs.CompatibilityList The task launch types the task definition was validated against. The valid values are MANAGED_INSTANCES, EC2, FARGATE, and EXTERNAL. For more information, see Amazon ECS launch types in the Amazon Elastic Container Service Developer Guide.
---@field cpu? aws.ecs.String The number of cpu units used by the task. If you use the EC2 launch type, this field is optional. Any value can be used. If you use the Fargate launch type, this field is required. You must use one of the following values. The value that you choose determines your range of valid values for the memory parameter.
---@field memory? aws.ecs.String The amount (in MiB) of memory used by the task.
---@field inferenceAccelerators? aws.ecs.InferenceAccelerators The Elastic Inference accelerator that's associated with the task.
---@field pidMode? aws.ecs.PidMode The process namespace to use for the containers in the task. The valid values are host or task. On Fargate for Linux containers, the only valid value is task. For example, monitoring sidecars might need pidMode to access information about other containers running in the same task.
---@field ipcMode? aws.ecs.IpcMode The IPC resource namespace to use for the containers in the task. The valid values are host, task, or none. If host is specified, then all containers within the tasks that specified the host IPC mode on the same container instance share the same IPC resources with the host Amazon EC2 instance. If task is specified, all containers within the specified task share the same IPC resources. If none is s...
---@field proxyConfiguration? aws.ecs.ProxyConfiguration The configuration details for the App Mesh proxy.
---@field registeredAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task definition was registered.
---@field deregisteredAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task definition was deregistered.
---@field deleteRequestedAt? aws.ecs.Timestamp The Unix timestamp for the time when the task definition delete was requested.
---@field registeredBy? aws.ecs.String The principal that registered the task definition.
---@field ephemeralStorage? aws.ecs.EphemeralStorage The ephemeral storage settings to use for tasks run with the task definition.
---@field enableFaultInjection? aws.ecs.BoxedBoolean Enables fault injection and allows for fault injection requests to be accepted from the task's containers. The default value is false.
---@field trustedExecutionConfiguration? aws.ecs.TrustedExecutionConfiguration The trusted execution configuration for tasks that use this task definition, including the isolation mode and, if configured, an attestation policy.

