---Information about a set of Amazon ECS tasks in either an CodeDeploy or an EXTERNAL deployment. An Amazon ECS task set includes details such as the desired number of tasks, how many tasks are running, and whether the task set serves production traffic.
---@class aws.ecs.TaskSet
---@field id? aws.ecs.String The ID of the task set.
---@field taskSetArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the task set.
---@field serviceArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the service the task set exists in.
---@field clusterArn? aws.ecs.String The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the cluster that the service that hosts the task set exists in.
---@field startedBy? aws.ecs.String The tag specified when a task set is started. If an CodeDeploy deployment created the task set, the startedBy parameter is CODE_DEPLOY. If an external deployment created the task set, the startedBy field isn't used.
---@field externalId? aws.ecs.String The external ID associated with the task set.
---@field status? aws.ecs.String The status of the task set. The following describes each state.
---@field taskDefinition? aws.ecs.String The task definition that the task set is using.
---@field computedDesiredCount aws.ecs.Integer The computed desired count for the task set. This is calculated by multiplying the service's desiredCount by the task set's scale percentage. The result is always rounded up. For example, if the computed desired count is 1.2, it rounds up to 2 tasks.
---@field pendingCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of tasks in the task set that are in the PENDING status during a deployment. A task in the PENDING state is preparing to enter the RUNNING state. A task set enters the PENDING status when it launches for the first time or when it's restarted after being in the STOPPED state.
---@field runningCount aws.ecs.Integer The number of tasks in the task set that are in the RUNNING status during a deployment. A task in the RUNNING state is running and ready for use.
